# Beat roll - the last bar retriggered at halving intervals, into the swap.
#
# The move every big-room set is built on, and the one thing this language could not say
# until `loop` existed: the leaving track's last beat caught and repeated, tighter and
# tighter, until it is a tone rather than a beat - and then it is gone and the new track
# is there.
#
# It is hooks rather than lanes because it is not a control moving. Nothing on a mixer
# does this; playback itself jumps backwards, which is `ab-loop` on the deck and nothing
# else. The fader underneath carries on exactly as it would have.

name    Beat roll
note    the last bar rolled into the swap
bars    8
align   beat
tempo   match
power   free

lane out gain
    bar 0    1.0    hold
    bar 6    1.0    smooth
    bar 7    0.0    hold
    bar 8    0.0    hold

# The arriving track builds underneath from half way, with no bottom of its own until the
# swap - so the roll is the only thing with any weight when it lands.
lane in gain
    bar 0    0.0    hold
    bar 4    0.0    power-up
    bar 7    1.0    hold
    bar 8    1.0    hold

lane in bass
    bar 0    -40    hold
    bar 7      0    hold
    bar 8      0    hold

lane in tempo
    bar 0    0.0    hold
    bar 7    0.0    smooth
    bar 8    1.0    hold

# Halving: half a bar, then a quarter, then an eighth - each one caught from wherever the
# playhead is by then, which is already inside the last one. `loop off` is not optional
# and not decoration: it is what hands the deck back.
hook bar 6      loop out 1/2
hook bar 6.5    loop out 1/4
hook bar 6.75   loop out 1/8
hook bar 7      loop off
hook bar 6      toast ⟲ rolling
